  "query_preview": "..\nToday through Thursday: An strong southerly upper level flow \npattern will continue to push warm, moist air across the region.\nWith the warmer air, snow levels will rise to above 6000 feet \nthrough the period. Rain will continue to through day with \nbrief break early Tuesday morning. A passing warm front will \nbring another round of widespread rain by the afternoon. \nAdditional amounts range up to a 0.10 of an inch across south-\ncentral and southeastern WA and the central ID Panhandle, \n0.10-0.20 of an inch for north- central and northeastern WA and \nthe northern ID Panhandle, 0.25-0.50 of an inch along the East \nSlopes of the Cascades, and 0.50-1.25 of an inch along the \ncrest. Washington Pass is the the only spot with possible snow. \nA range of 1 to 4 inches is possible through Tuesday night. The \nrains could impact recent burn scars in the Cascades. A low \nthreat of debris flows and/or flooding is possible. \n\nWednesday and Thursday will have a ridge pushing into the \nregion for Southwest Conus. It will press the moisture plume \nwest and north bringing a drying trend to the region. Residual \nmoisture in the boundary layer is expected to bring an increase \nin fog and low ceilings for valleys and portions of the Basin. \nHighs for the period will be in the 50s to low 60s. Lows will be\nin the upper 30s and 40s.\n\nFriday through Monday: The ridge axis shifts East allowing for \na series of Lows to push through the region. The period will \ntrend cooler with snow levels dropping to around 5000 ft by \nSaturday morning. Cascade Passes can expect snow. Stevens having\na 25-75 percent probability of 2 to 5 inches and Washington \nPass with a possible 5 to 13 inches from Friday into Monday. \nHighs for the period will be in the 40s to low 50s. Lows will be\nin the upper 20s and 30s. /JDC",
